ally, without unnecessary expansion or empty areas. It\'s crucial to maintain compact density for elements like card padding, chart heights, table row heights, and sidebar width. The dashboard needs full-width layouts with compact components and fluid columns, avoiding narrow centered sections on larger displays.\n\n\n**Re-evaluating Dashboard Sections**\n\nI\'m reconsidering the arrangement of dashboard sections, particularly the \'Live Investigation Activity\' and \'Geolocation\' components, within the existing four-column layout. I\'ve realized stacking them in the right column creates a height imbalance relative to the left column. I\'m referencing section 8 of the prompt to ensure the layout adapts naturally and uses space efficiently. I am re-reading the guidelines to avoid redesigning, resizing elements excessively, or creating unnecessary whitespace, while adhering to the suggested desktop structure, as appropriate for the current implementation in `SecurityOverviewPage.tsx`.\n\n\n\n**Analyzing Grid Columns**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the grid column behavior, particularly in the `SecurityOverviewPage.tsx`, to see how the layout responds on larger displays like 1920px or even ultra-wide monitors. I\'m focusing on the effect of `lg:col-span-8` and `lg:col-span-4`, and whether the columns are truly fluid, given the container\'s `max-w-7xl mx-auto` constraints. I\'m also delving into how individual components like `DynamicEmailVolumeGraph` adapt to varying widths, especially if their containers expand. It seems the container is stuck at 1280px.\n\n\n"
