  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing App Structure**\n\nI\'m currently dissecting the application\'s top-level structure. Right now, I see `App.tsx` handling direct routing to pages like `SecurityOverviewPage`, `CaseInboxPage`, and others. These pages are all wrapped by `AppShell`. I\'m breaking down `AppShell`, which renders the `Sidebar`, `AppTopBar`, and the main content area for the current page.\n\n\n**Fixing Layout Responsiveness**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the core issue: the fixed-width containers causing excessive whitespace. I\'ve pinpointed problematic patterns like `max-w-7xl mx-auto` and I\'m planning to replace these with more fluid designs. The goal is to maximize viewport utilization while preserving the existing visual density. I\'m focusing on ensuring the dashboard expands horizontally to consume available screen space. I\'m carefully auditing for these fixed-width attributes, which currently restrict the application\'s responsiveness.\n\n\n**Adjusting Screen Consumption**\n\nI\'m now focusing on optimizing the application for a variety of screen sizes, particularly large monitors. I\'m prioritizing available viewport usage, while taking the sidebar into account. On very large displays, I\'m opting for reasonable page padding, as opposed to a narrow, centered layout. The goal is to avoid unnecessary whitespace and maintain a professional interface that doesn\'t feel overly spacious. The main content will take full width and expand to fill available space, with fluid padding.\n\n\n**Optimizing Screen Real Estate**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on screen-specific layouts. My focus is on ensuring the dashboard adapts seamlessly across resolutions: 1920px and above, it will use the full width; not remain centered, leaving empty margins. The layout is going to be full-width, compact components, with fluid columns. I\'m also ensuring the sidebar stays fixed width on desktops, collapses on tablets and converts to a drawer on mobile to optimize horizontal space.\n\n\n**Examining Page Layout**\n\nI\'m now diving into the `SecurityOverviewPage.tsx` middle section\'s layout. Specifically, I\'m analyzing how sections are arranged, focusing on the 4 sections in the middle. I\'m taking a look at how the grid is set up, where the left side is taking up 8 columns and the right 4. I\'m focusing on why a grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 is present.\n\n\n**Revising Section Layout**\n\nI\'m now revising the layout of the `SecurityOverviewPage`, specifically the middle section. I\'ve pinpointed an excessive vertical gap on the left side due to unequal row heights between the left and right columns. The current right section, with two stacked cards, is taller than the single-row section on the left. I\'m exploring layout options to achieve consistent row heights. I\'m thinking of equal-width columns for Threat Overview, Email Volume Trend, and Live Activity, then determining the best placement for Geolocation. I am also considering the user prompt for inspiration on layout.\n\n\n**Reorganizing Card Placement**\n\nI\'m now determining the best location for the Geolocation card within the `SecurityOverviewPage`. I\'m comparing whether Geolocation should be part of a grid with the other sections. I\'m considering side-by-side positioning with Threat Overview and Email Volume Trend, or potentially placing it under those sections. I\'m also analyzing the original placement of Geolocation in the right column and evaluating the current author\'s prompt, along with the user\'s diagram. I am considering several options and weighing the pros and cons of each, including options for Live Investigation Activity.\n\n\n**Evaluating Card Placement**\n\nI\'m now evaluating the impact of card layout adjustments within the SecurityOverviewPage. I\'ve realized that putting Threat Overview, Email Volume Trend, and Live Activity side-by-side on wide screens is a good approach. The key observation is their consistent height of approximately 360px each, which means this approach will result in even sizing when laid out in columns. This makes the layout neat and well-balanced.\n\n\n**Determining Section Arrangement**\n\nI\'m now determining the ideal placement of the \"Geolocation of Malicious Senders\" card in the SecurityOverviewPage layout, now that I\'ve noticed they are nearly the same height. I\'m focusing on whether they should be side-by-side with Recent Cases, on wider screens (Recent Cases: 8 cols, Geolocation: 4 cols) to form a balanced, compact row, or if they should be placed elsewhere. I\'m considering their respective contents and how they best complement each other within a SOC dashboard context, including their heights. The bottom row: Top Threat Types, Top Targeted Brands, AI Assistant.\n\n\n**Confirming Height Alignment**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the AI Assistant card\'s height, roughly 330px, aligning it perfectly with other sections. I\'m now verifying the proposed grid composition. It appears to match the original author\'s intent, with compact, consistent heights across rows. The analytics row aligns perfectly with the user\'s prompt. Recent Cases\' span on desktop provides a comfortable viewing experience.\n\n\n**Considering Geolocation Placement**\n\nI\'m now integrating the user\'s explicit prompt into my card arrangement analysis, and it\'s clarifying Geolocation\'s ideal position. The user\'s diagram offers a comprehensive blueprint. The \"Geolocation of Malicious Senders\" card could pair with \"Recent Cases\" - using grid columns (8, 4) on larger screens to maintain a balanced layout. This also offers space for adding it full-width on smaller screens. This approach seems to resonate most closely with the user\'s intent, and the logical grouping of data within a SOC dashboard.\n\n\n**Assessing Screen Resolution**\n\nI\'m now determining responsive breakpoints for \"Recent Cases\" and \"Geolocation\". I\'ve analyzed column widths at various resolutions: at 1024px, the 8-column layout for Recent Cases is too narrow without horizontal scrolling. However, on larger screens like 1920px, there is more than enough room for the table. I\'m thinking of placing it full-width on smaller screens. I\'m investigating alternative placements for Geolocation to improve the layout. I am also considering why the original layout had issues.\n\n\n**Refining Layout Strategy**\n\nI\'m now refining the layout strategy for the SecurityOverviewPage, specifically focusing on the bottom rows. I\'m evaluating two core options: first, pairing Recent Cases with Geolocation in a grid, and second, integrating Geolocation with Top Threat Types, Top Targeted Brands, and the AI Assistant. I\'ve realized if I can make 3 cards in Row 2, then I can also fit 4 cards in Row 4. I\'m focusing on the best way to leverage the existing grid structure and maintain visual balance. I need to make the Geolocation card responsive, given that it will now be on a row with other cards. I\'m carefully considering how each option impacts screen real estate and the user\'s experience.\n\n\n\n**Evaluating Card Placement**\n\nI\'m now determining how Geolocation will be placed. I am evaluating a new approach. I\'m focusing on whether Geolocation can fit in 1/4 of a 4-column row. At 1920px (400px), Geolocation fits well, but is too small at 1024px. I\'m considering new grid layout options, and what if I keep Threat Overview + Email Volume Trend + Live Activity in the second row? I\'m evaluating at `md` and `xl` resolutions, and Recent Cases and Geolocation.\n\n\n**Confirming Visual Harmony**\n\nI\'ve just validated a harmonious layout, row by row. Recent Cases and Geolocation\'s nearly identical heights offer pixel-perfect alignment, both full-width on smaller screens and side-by-side on larger screens. The 3-column arrangement in the final row maintains balance. I believe this setup delivers a balanced, purposeful and consistent layout. Every row has a consistent, balanced arrangement, and all rows have equal heights.\n\n\n**Validating User Prompt**\n\nI\'m now diving into the user prompt to solidify the Geolocation card arrangement. I\'m focusing on their key requirement: utilizing available width efficiently. I\'m taking a close look at the suggested structure, but carefully considering that the explicit instruction is not to force the original exact structure if the existing dashboard is better, and considering there is no horizontal overflow.\n\nI\'ve got the cards\' heights and horizontal space usage optimized. I know that by placing Geolocation and Recent Cases together, I can optimize available horizontal space. The current solution seems to match the user\'s requirements for responsive design, without the risk of any horizontal overflows.\n\n\n**Auditing Graph Behavior**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the dynamic email volume graph scales within its container. The `preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"` attribute in `DynamicEmailVolumeGraph.tsx` is stretching the SVG, which could be problematic. I\'m assessing if adjusting this attribute or modifying the `VIEW_WIDTH` and `VIEW_HEIGHT` dynamically would improve responsiveness. I\'m also ensuring the graph\'s data points render correctly across various screen sizes and prevent distortion.\n\n\n**Optimizing Graph Scaling**\n\nI\'m now investigating the scaling of the dynamic email volume graph and its data points to avoid distortion. The current use of `preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"` is creating elliptical shapes for the data circles, and I want to fix this. My approach involves making the SVG responsive, either dynamically adjusting `VIEW_WIDTH` based on the container size or utilizing `vector-effect=\"non-scaling-stroke\"` and recomputing points. I\'m focusing on utilizing `ResizeObserver` for dynamic width.\n\n\n**Validating Graph Responsiveness**\n\nI\'ve confirmed the email volume graph\'s responsiveness: it now scales smoothly across all screen sizes without distortion. Pixel-perfect tooltip positioning is maintained. Using a `ResizeObserver` for dynamic width adjustments and removing `preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"` delivers high-quality, responsive SVG data visualization, proving far superior to the previous approach.\n\n\n**Refining Map Display**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the `ThreatWorldMap` component. I\'ve noticed how its SVG\'s fixed `viewBox` and height constraints (`h-44 sm:h-48`) create empty space on wider screens. I\'m focused on making it responsive, by using `aspect-ratio: 2/1`, `w-full` and `h-auto` to dynamically adjust the map\'s size to the container width. I\'ll test these changes, and check the associated statistics.\n\n\n**Refining Progress Bars**\n\nI\'m now focusing on optimizing the progress bars across the application. I found that previously, fixed pixel widths restricted the bars. I\'m fixing this by changing `w-24` to `flex-1` with a maximum width in the progress bar. This way, the bar will dynamically adapt, and maintain visual consistency across different screen sizes. I\'m also ensuring the threat overview and risk posture banners are fully responsive with consistent visual styling.\n\n\n**Refining Component Responsiveness**\n\nI\'m now integrating the `flex-1` attribute to optimize the `Live Investigation Activity Card`\'s width, preventing overflow. I\'ve also examined the `Recent Cases Table`\'s code to ensure a fluid layout. I\'m focusing on ensuring components dynamically adapt to different screen sizes, truncating text as needed. My current focus is to ensure the layout remains visually consistent.\n\n\n**Improving Table Usability**\n\nI\'m now focusing on optimizing the Recent Cases table. I\'ve identified that the `max-w-xs` constraint on the subject column prematurely truncates text on wider screens, violating visual behavior requirements. I\'m updating to `max-w-lg` or similar to allow for more horizontal information, improving readability and analyst usability. I am ensuring that the table maximizes horizontal screen space.\n\n\n**Revising Row Organization**\n\nI\'m now determining how to effectively arrange the bottom rows, specifically evaluating the combination of three cards in one row, versus a balanced four-card grid. After reviewing the user\'s prompt and diagram, and the layout for the other cards, I believe a 3-column arrangement, in the same style of top cards, is the most effective choice. I\'m focusing on the logical order and visual harmony of the content in each section.\n\n\n**Finalizing Component Layout**\n\nI\'m now implementing the proposed 3-column arrangement for the bottom sections, maintaining consistency with the top. I\'m prioritizing available width utilization, as the user\'s prompt indicated, and making sure the existing dashboard has a better composition. I\'m confirming that the Geolocation card pairs with Recent Cases and the three-column approach in Row 2. The entire layout has now been validated, and the page is optimized.\n\n\n**Evaluating Layout Alternatives**\n\nI\'m now considering the implications of full-width \"Recent Cases\" on very large screens, and reconsidering the column layout. Expanding the table introduces awkward spacing in the columns. A smaller, readable table layout, combined with the \"Geolocation\" side-by-side, offers the best experience.\n\n\n**Assessing Row Placement**\n\nI\'m now determining ideal row placements, and I\'m currently assessing the four-column layout. I\'ve calculated available space at various resolutions, confirming 4-column layouts fit comfortably on wider screens, but are too cramped on smaller ones. I\'m focusing on responsiveness, evaluating alternative arrangements, and determining where the cards best fit in the existing structure, specifically Geolocation in relation to other cards.\n\n\n**Interpreting User Intent**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the user\'s intent. The prompt highlights sharing rows for visual balance and maximizing space efficiency, especially for \"Threat Overview\" and \"Email Volume Trend.\" They prefer the current structure, as long as horizontal space is optimized, with a layout that does not force the exact structure, and respects current compositions.\n\n\n**Analyzing Layout Issues**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a specific layout inconsistency within the `SecurityOverviewPage`. I\'ve realized the original grid structure caused uneven row heights between the left and right columns due to the placement of Live Activity and Geolocation. I\'m prioritizing equalizing heights to fix a vertical whitespace issue and ensuring visual harmony, and am deciding how to most efficiently rearrange these elements to maintain optimal screen space.\n\n\n**Validating Grid Arrangement**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a new grid structure that should result in consistent row heights. I\'ve calculated that the left and right columns can have 100% equal heights, at 744px each. With the layout: Threat Overview, Email Volume Trend, Recent Cases on the left, and Live Investigation Activity, and Geolocation on the right, the heights are now equivalent. I am using a full-width, three-column layout in the bottom row.\n\n\n**Validating Final Structure**\n\nI\'m now implementing the calculated grid structure. With the KPI cards at the top, followed by the middle operation grid and bottom analytics, everything now aligns. The key is in the equal-height left and right columns within the operations grid, resolving the uneven vertical space. The final touches are on the bottom row, with equal columns. The layout now aligns perfectly, and is balanced across a variety of screen sizes.\n\n\n**Confirming Full Balance**\n\nI\'m now achieving precise height alignment and full visual balance across all screen sizes. Recent Cases, with 8 columns, nicely expands the table, and Geolocation, with 4 columns, creates a logical pairing. The structure addresses the prompt\'s request for horizontal expansion without layout overflow. I\'m focusing on ensuring consistent row heights and balanced columns.\n\n\n**Evaluating Layout Structure**\n\nI\'m now thinking about extremely large screens; specifically, 2560px. The 8-column layout for \"Recent Cases\" and the 4-column layout for \"Geolocation\" are both roomy. Both columns elegantly fill the screen. I\'m also now comparing this layout to the user\'s diagram, which leads to Structure 1. Structure 1 is KPI, Threat, Recent Cases, and finally the Analytics. I\'m now comparing both layouts.\n\n\n**Comparing Layouts**\n\nI\'m now weighing the merits of Structure 1 versus Structure 2, using the user\'s diagram. I\'m noting Structure 1\'s key layout of Threat Overview, Email Volume Trend, and Activity in the same row, questioning Geolocation\'s place. I\'m revisiting the user\'s diagram to analyze the placement of Geolocation.\n\n\n**Comparing Layouts**\n\nI\'m now weighing the merits of Structure 1, from the user diagram, against Structure 2, found in the code, and considering the prompt, which emphasizes using available width efficiently. Structure 2 seems to be better suited, optimizing screen real estate. The diagram\'s composition is being carefully assessed.\n\n\n**Refining Column Organization**\n\nI\'m now revising the column layout based on insights gained. Previously mis-placed, `Recent Cases` is now seamlessly integrated under `Threat Overview` and `Email Volume Trend` within an 8-column span, harmonizing heights. On smaller screens, they stack; on larger, they sit side-by-side. With `Live Investigation Activity` and `Geolocation` below it, both sides align vertically. The bottom row follows.\n\n\n**Evaluating Table Width**\n\nI\'m now analyzing the Recent Cases table\'s responsiveness across different screen sizes. I\'ve realized at medium screen sizes the horizontal scrollbar is required, while on larger screens, the table has more than enough space. I\'m focusing on why a 12-column span isn\'t appropriate at `lg` resolutions, and what to do with section 2. I\'m also ensuring the table fits comfortably without unnecessary horizontal scrolling on larger displays. I\'m checking the current column widths to refine the table\'s sizing.\n\n\n**Refining Final Layout**\n\nI\'m now implementing a new layout based on aligning the email volume trend, threat overview and live activity, which aligns the existing diagram, and solves prior issues. My calculations show a balanced 3-column layout will address all previous inconsistencies, maintaining visual harmony on all screen sizes, while matching the user\'s diagrams.\n\n\n**Confirming User-Provided Layout**\n\nI\'m now implementing the explicit row organization from the user\'s diagram. It\'s becoming evident that combining \"Threat Overview,\" \"Email Volume Trend,\" and \"Live Activity\" in a 3-column arrangement directly aligns with their suggested structure. I\'m focusing on \"Recent Cases\" alongside Geolocation, and how to best use the bottom row. I believe I am meeting all user requests with this structure.\n\n\n**Validating Final Arrangement**\n\nI\'m now solidifying the layout with Recent Cases and Geolocation. At wider resolutions, they form a balanced side-by-side arrangement, offering good space for both table and map. Smaller screens will have the cards full-width. This approach optimizes horizontal space and aligns perfectly with how the application should look.\n\n\n\n**Validating Balanced Structure**\n\nI\'m now implementing a new grid, with three equal-width columns at the bottom: Threat Overview, Targeted Brands, and AI Assistant. This approach ensures consistent heights across rows and leverages all available screen space, aligning with the user\'s focus on density. The final layout feels balanced and the user\'s intention is satisfied. The overall design now appears harmonious and easy to read.\n\n\n**Validating Responsive Harmony**\n\nI\'m now achieving pixel-perfect alignment: consistent heights across rows and balanced column arrangements are the current focus. The layout adapts smoothly, and it addresses horizontal space efficiently. I\'ve ensured a visually cohesive arrangement with no wasted whitespace, for a great user experience.\n\n\n**Analyzing Card Styles**\n\nI\'m now focusing on ensuring consistent styling across all cards for visual harmony. Every card shares `bg-white rounded-xl p-5 border border-slate-200/80 shadow-xs flex flex-col justify-between`, establishing a uniform appearance. I\'ve confirmed matching padding, borders, shadows, and flexbox alignment within cards and am evaluating the prompt on design tokens.\n\n\n**Defining Design Tokens**\n\nI\'m now focusing on centralizing design decisions into reusable CSS variables. I\'m going to add variables for common values like padding, card radius, and component widths to `src/index.css`. This will enable consistent styling and easier theming. Currently, the `src/index.css` is an `@import \"tailwindcss\";` - so let\'s set this up.\n\n\n**Refactoring Container Styles**\n\nI\'m now focusing on removing hardcoded maximum widths and centering from various placeholder components to improve their responsiveness. I\'m swapping instances of `max-w-7xl mx-auto` and `max-w-4xl mx-auto` with more fluid alternatives. My objective is to ensure containers fully utilize available screen width while retaining appropriate padding and spacing, as well as optimizing the `AppTopBar.tsx` and `Sidebar.tsx`.\n\n\n**Refining Sidebar Behavior**\n\nI\'m now implementing responsive behavior for the sidebar across different devices. My focus is on ensuring it adheres to the user\'s specifications: fixed width on desktops and drawer or collapsed state on smaller screens. I am making it hidden off-screen on mobile and tablet with an overlay backdrop, and investigating the existing application architecture to determine feasibility. On tablets, the sidebar collapses into an icon-only rail.\n\n\n**Implementing Responsive Sidebar**\n\nI\'ve just added the responsive sidebar logic, as a drawer on mobile and tablet, and fixed on desktop. The hamburger menu toggles a slide-out drawer on smaller screens, while desktop displays a fixed sidebar. The design remains untouched on large screens, while on mobile and tablet, the dashboard takes full width now! I\'m verifying this in the `ui-store.ts` file.\n\n\n**Refining Search Bar**\n\nI\'m now implementing responsiveness for the search bar within the top header, making it fluid across various screen sizes. The goal is for the search bar to expand horizontally within available space, rather than remaining unnecessarily narrow. I\'m focusing on ensuring this responsiveness, without overflow issues, using `display:flex; align-items:center; gap:...` for its layout. I am also making sure to add a `Search` icon.\n\n\n\n**Refining Search Bar Responsiveness**\n\nI\'ve just refined the search bar, ensuring responsiveness across devices. Using `w-52 md:w-64 lg:w-80 xl:w-96 2xl:w-[28rem]`, it now expands on larger screens without overflow, and stays compact on small screens. I\'ve also implemented flexible header padding and made the search icon responsive.\n\n\n**Adjusting Graph Responsiveness**\n\nI\'m now integrating a responsive graph. I\'m using `ResizeObserver` with a container `ref` to dynamically adjust the graph\'s `VIEW_WIDTH` based on available screen space, preventing SVG distortion, and ensuring data is displayed properly across different screen sizes. I have a `max` width, so the data is displayed properly.\n\n\n**Validating Graph Refinement**\n\nI\'ve just validated the email graph\'s responsiveness: the points and curve spline are now dynamically computed for any `VIEW_WIDTH` via `ResizeObserver`, ensuring the chart scales perfectly without distortion. The SVG `viewBox` matches the container width, so there\'s zero horizontal distortion, and tooltips are now pixel perfect. The anomaly ring pulses are truly circular.\n\n\n**Validating Chart Responsiveness**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that the email graph dynamically resizes, resolving distortion, and rendering correctly, at 1024. `ResizeObserver` works smoothly and the `viewBox` is fine, matching container width, and with perfect tooltips. No further anomalies exist, as all checks are clear, and horizontal distortion is eliminated.\n\n\n\n**Confirming Column Harmony**\n\nI\'m now assessing the layout for consistency across screen sizes. I can confirm equal-height columns, matching styling and zero vertical disparity within the three-column layout. The bottom row\'s operations section, with the Recent Cases table and Geolocation, is now under review. I\'m focusing on ensuring its responsiveness and visual balance with the upper sections. I\'m thinking about the user\'s diagrams.\n\n\n**Validating Section Alignment**\n\nI\'m now implementing the calculated responsive section organization. Both the Recent Cases and Geolocation section are designed with 1/3, 2/3 viewports, for 560px, 3440px. I\'ve modified the subject column, from `max-w-xs`, to `max-w-lg truncate` for larger monitors. The Geolocation card now matches `Recent Cases` in height.\n\n\n"
